(window.UPANISHAD_DATA=window.UPANISHAD_DATA||{})["taittiriya"]={"slug":"taittiriya","name":"Taittirīya","deva":"तैत्तिरीय","note":"","intro":["A principal (mukhya) Upaniṣad of the Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda, forming Prapāṭhakas 7–9 of the Taittirīya Āraṇyaka. It is counted among the ten Upaniṣads commented upon by Ādi Śaṅkara and is one of the most-studied texts of Vedānta. The name derives from the Taittirīya śākhā (recension) of the Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda; tradition (Viṣṇu Purāṇa) traces the school to pupils of Yāska's pupil who received the Veda as tittiri (partridges) — hence taittirīya.","Sanskrit text source: sanskritdocuments.org (tait.itx, transliterated by Avinash Sathaye &amp; Kartik, proofread by John Manetta et al.). Accent-marked version (taitaccent.itx) and Sāyaṇa's Taittirīya-Āraṇyaka-bhāṣya consulted for word-division. Original text is in the public domain; all transliteration, glosses, translations, and commentary digests below are this compiler's own work."],"sections":[{"t":"Introduction","u":[{"ref":"Taittirīya Upaniṣad","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["तैत्तिरीय उपनिषत् — \"The Upaniṣad of the (school of the) Partridges\"","A principal (mukhya) Upaniṣad of the Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda, forming Prapāṭhakas 7–9 of the Taittirīya Āraṇyaka. It is counted among the ten Upaniṣads commented upon by Ādi Śaṅkara and is one of the most-studied texts of Vedānta. The name derives from the Taittirīya śākhā (recension) of the Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda; tradition (Viṣṇu Purāṇa) traces the school to pupils of Yāska's pupil who received the Veda as tittiri (partridges) — hence taittirīya.","Sanskrit text source: sanskritdocuments.org (tait.itx, transliterated by Avinash Sathaye &amp; Kartik, proofread by John Manetta et al.). Accent-marked version (taitaccent.itx) and Sāyaṇa's Taittirīya-Āraṇyaka-bhāṣya consulted for word-division. Original text is in the public domain; all transliteration, glosses, translations, and commentary digests below are this compiler's own work."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Structure of the whole Upaniṣad","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Vallī</td><td>Common names</td><td>Anuvākas</td><td>Core subject</td></tr><tr><td>I</td><td>Śīkṣā-vallī (Saṃhitī-upaniṣad)</td><td>12</td><td>Phonetics, upāsanā (meditations on combinations, vyāhṛtis, Oṃ), the ethical convocation. The \"ritual–meditative\" gate.</td></tr><tr><td>II</td><td>Brahmānanda-vallī / Ānanda-vallī</td><td>9</td><td>Brahma-vidyā proper: the five sheaths (pañca-kośa) and the calculus of bliss (ānanda-mīmāṃsā).</td></tr><tr><td>III</td><td>Bhṛgu-vallī (Bhārgavī Vāruṇī Vidyā)</td><td>10</td><td>Bhṛgu's progressive enquiry into Brahman as anna → prāṇa → manas → vijñāna → ānanda; food-ethics; the closing song of the knower.</td></tr></table>","This document covers Vallī I (Śīkṣā-vallī). Vallīs II and III follow in subsequent instalments, appended to this same file."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"The five commentaries summarised","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. Ādi Śaṅkara (c. 700 CE) — Taittirīyopaniṣad-bhāṣya, the foundational Advaita commentary and the lens through which the text is most often read. Treats Śīkṣā-vallī's meditations as karma-miśra upāsanā (worship blended with ritual) that purifies the mind for the brahma-vidyā of Vallī II; reads the convocation (anuv. 11) as the gṛhastha-dharma that precedes renunciation.","2. Sureśvarācārya (9th c.) — Taittirīya-vārttika (a vast verse sub-commentary on Śaṅkara's bhāṣya, with the Vārtika-sāra tradition). Defends Śaṅkara line-by-line against ritualist (Mīmāṃsā) and dualist objections; especially detailed on why the upāsanās are not themselves liberating but preparatory.","3. Rangarāmānuja (17th c.) — Prakāśikā on the Upaniṣads in the Viśiṣṭādvaita (Rāmānuja) school. Reads the same passages theistically: the \"Puruṣa in the heart-ākāśa\" (anuv. 6) is Para-Brahman Nārāyaṇa as inner controller (antaryāmin), not a nirguṇa absolute; the vyāhṛtis and saṃhitās are upāsanās on the Lord's vibhūti (glory).","4. The Advaita-Ashrama reading-tradition (Swami Gambhīrānanda's standard English rendering &amp; class-notes; represented here in this compiler's paraphrase) — the careful, literal, Śaṅkara-faithful translator's voice: clarifies anvaya (construal), settles which clause governs which, and flags where the ritual sense and the meditative sense diverge.","5. Sri Aurobindo (early 20th c.; The Upanishads / Kena and Other Upanishads) — integral / psychological reading (in this compiler's paraphrase). Treats the vallī as a graded discipline of consciousness: phonetics → the powers of the word → the ascent of being; reads \"satyaṃ vada\" and the bliss-formulas as descriptions of a self-exceeding evolution rather than mere injunctions.","On usage below: under each anuvāka, Śaṅkara gives the classical Advaita construal; Sureśvara the polemical/defensive depth; Rangarāmānuja the Viśiṣṭādvaita (theistic) alternative; Gambhīrānanda-tradition the literal translator's settling of sense; Aurobindo the psychological-evolutionary angle. Digests are in this compiler's words, not verbatim quotations."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Vallī I","title":"Śīkṣā-vallī","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Śīkṣā-vallī (\"the creeper/chapter on phonetics\") opens the Upaniṣad with the science of correct recitation, then ascends through a graded series of upāsanās (contemplative worships) and closes with the famous convocation address. In Śaṅkara's framing it is the sādhana-vallī: it purifies and steadies the seeker so that the brahma-vidyā of the next vallī can land.","Each anuvāka is given as: Devanāgarī → IAST → pada/key-term gloss → literal → paraphrase → visual → practical steps → 5-commentary digest.","Grammar abbreviations: m./f./n. = gender; nom./acc./instr./dat./abl./gen./loc./voc. = case; sg./du./pl. = number; pres./impf./impv./pot./ger./inf./caus. = tense/mood/non-finite; indecl. = indeclinable."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Anuvāka 1","title":"Śānti-pāṭha: invocation to the cosmic powers","sa":["ॐ शं नो मित्रः शं वरुणः । शं नो भवत्वर्यमा ।","शं न इन्द्रो बृहस्पतिः । शं नो विष्णुरुरुक्रमः ।","नमो ब्रह्मणे । नमस्ते वायो । त्वमेव प्रत्यक्षं ब्रह्मासि ।","त्वामेव प्रत्यक्षं ब्रह्म वदिष्यामि । ऋतं वदिष्यामि ।","सत्यं वदिष्यामि । तन्मामवतु । तद्वक्तारमवतु ।","अवतु माम् । अवतु वक्तारम् ॥","ॐ शान्तिः शान्तिः शान्तिः ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["oṃ śaṃ no mitraḥ śaṃ varuṇaḥ | śaṃ no bhavatv aryamā |","śaṃ na indro bṛhaspatiḥ | śaṃ no viṣṇur urukramaḥ |","namo brahmaṇe | namas te vāyo | tvam eva pratyakṣaṃ brahmāsi |","tvām eva pratyakṣaṃ brahma vadiṣyāmi | ṛtaṃ vadiṣyāmi |","satyaṃ vadiṣyāmi | tan mām avatu | tad vaktāram avatu |","avatu mām | avatu vaktāram || oṃ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ || 1 ||"],"words":[["śam","indecl.","(be) auspicious / a source of well-being"],["naḥ","pron. dat./gen. pl.","to us"],["mitraḥ","m. nom. sg.","Mitra (deity of day / friendship)"],["varuṇaḥ","m. nom. sg.","Varuṇa (deity of cosmic order, waters)"],["bhavatu","3 sg. impv. of bhū","may (he) be"],["aryamā","m. nom. sg.","Aryaman (deity of compacts)"],["indraḥ","m. nom. sg.","Indra"],["bṛhaspatiḥ","m. nom. sg.","Bṛhaspati (lord of sacred speech)"],["viṣṇuḥ uru-kramaḥ","m. nom. sg. + adj.","Viṣṇu, the wide-striding"],["namaḥ","n. nom. sg.","salutation"],["brahmaṇe","n. dat. sg.","to Brahman"],["te","pron. dat. sg.","to you"],["vāyo","m. voc. sg.","O Vāyu"],["tvam eva","pron. + emph.","you alone"],["pratyakṣam","adv./adj.","directly perceptible, manifest"],["brahma asi","n. + 2 sg. pres.","are Brahman"],["vadiṣyāmi","1 sg. fut. of vad","I shall declare"],["ṛtam","n. acc. sg.","the cosmic order / truth-in-thought"],["satyam","n. acc. sg.","truth(-in-speech)"],["tat","pron. n. acc. sg.","that"],["mām avatu","pron. + 3 sg. impv.","may (it) protect me"],["vaktāram","m. acc. sg.","the speaker (teacher)"],["śāntiḥ","f. nom. sg.","peace"]],"lit":"\"Oṃ. May Mitra be well-being to us; may Varuṇa be well-being; may Aryaman be well-being to us; may Indra and Bṛhaspati, may wide-striding Viṣṇu be well-being to us. Salutation to Brahman. Salutation to you, O Vāyu. You indeed are the directly-perceptible Brahman. You alone I shall call the manifest Brahman. I shall speak the right (ṛta); I shall speak the true (satya). May that protect me; may that protect the teacher. May it protect me; may it protect the teacher. Oṃ, peace, peace, peace.\"","para":"Before study begins, the student invokes the cosmic powers for harmony, then singles out Vāyu as the immediately experienced Brahman (the breath/air one directly feels), vowing to speak ṛta (truth as ordered cognition) and satya (truth as faithful speech). The triple \"peace\" wards off the three sources of disturbance — ādhyātmika (self), ādhibhautika (beings), ādhidaivika (cosmic).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","Vāyu is named \"manifest Brahman\" because, among the elements, air is the most pervasive and directly sensed; the invocation is adhyayana-vighna-śānti (removing obstacles to study). Ṛta = the mental resolve of truth, satya = its expression in word and deed."],["Sureśvara","stresses that calling Vāyu \"Brahman\" is pratīka-upāsanā (worship through a symbol), not a final ontological claim; the symbol is a foothold for a mind not yet able to grasp the unconditioned."],["Rangarāmānuja","the deities are real powers governed by the indwelling Lord; \"you alone are Brahman\" addresses the antaryāmin who ensouls Vāyu — peace is sought from the Lord through His cosmic agents."],["Gambhīrānanda-tradition","fixes the construal of tat (\"that,\" i.e. the Brahman just addressed) as the subject of both avatu clauses; the doubling (me / the teacher, twice) is emphatic, not redundant."],["Sri Aurobindo","the gods are powers of the one Consciousness; the prayer aligns the seeker's instruments (mind = ṛta, speech = satya) so that knowledge can descend without distortion."]],"notes":["Visual — the invocation's logic","<table><tr><td>Movement</td><td>Content</td><td>Function</td></tr><tr><td>1. Outward (devas)</td><td>Mitra, Varuṇa, Aryaman, Indra, Bṛhaspati, Viṣṇu</td><td>Harmonise the field of study</td></tr><tr><td>2. Inward (Vāyu)</td><td>\"You alone are the manifest Brahman\"</td><td>Locate Brahman in direct experience (breath)</td></tr><tr><td>3. Vow</td><td>ṛtaṃ vadiṣyāmi · satyaṃ vadiṣyāmi</td><td>Bind cognition and speech to truth</td></tr><tr><td>4. Protection</td><td>tan mām avatu · tad vaktāram avatu</td><td>Shield both pupil and teacher</td></tr><tr><td>5. Śāntiḥ × 3</td><td>ādhyātmika · ādhibhautika · ādhidaivika</td><td>Still the three disturbances</td></tr></table>","Practical steps / observations","•  Recite as a genuine saṅkalpa before any study or meditation session — it sets intention (truth) and asks for harmony, not results.","•  Note the move from many devas to the one Vāyu: a contemplative drill of converging plurality into the single, directly-felt breath.","•  Take the three śāntis as a checklist: settle inner agitation, then friction with others, then anxiety about what you cannot control."]}]},{"t":"","u":[{"ref":"Anuvāka 2","title":"Śīkṣā: the science of recitation","sa":["ॐ शीक्षां व्याख्यास्यामः । वर्णः स्वरः । मात्रा बलम् ।","साम सन्तानः । इत्युक्तः शीक्षाध्यायः ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["oṃ śīkṣāṃ vyākhyāsyāmaḥ | varṇaḥ svaraḥ | mātrā balam |","sāma santānaḥ | ity uktaḥ śīkṣādhyāyaḥ || 1 ||"],"words":[["śīkṣām","f. acc. sg.","the phonetic science (of pronunciation)"],["vyākhyāsyāmaḥ","1 pl. fut. of vi-ā-khyā","we shall expound"],["varṇaḥ","m. nom. sg.","letter / phoneme"],["svaraḥ","m. nom. sg.","accent, pitch (udātta/anudātta/svarita)"],["mātrā","f. nom. sg.","quantity, duration (short/long/prolated)"],["balam","n. nom. sg.","force / effort of articulation"],["sāma","n. nom. sg.","evenness, uniformity (of tone)"],["santānaḥ","m. nom. sg.","conjunction, continuity (euphonic combination)"],["iti uktaḥ","indecl. + m. nom. sg. pp.","thus has been stated"],["śīkṣā-adhyāyaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the chapter on phonetics"]],"lit":"\"Oṃ. We shall expound Śīkṣā: letter, accent, quantity, strength, evenness, conjunction — (thus) the chapter on Śīkṣā has been stated.\"","para":"The Upaniṣad names the six elements of Śīkṣā (the Vedāṅga of phonetics). Right knowledge must rest on right speech, and right speech on these six: varṇa (the phoneme itself), svara (its pitch-accent), mātrā (its duration), bala (the articulatory effort/place), sāma (evenness of delivery), santāna (the unbroken euphonic chain, sandhi).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the six are stated only in saṅgraha (summary); the detailed Śīkṣā treatises (Prātiśākhyas) elaborate them. The point here is that brahma-jñāna is transmitted orally, so the medium must be flawless."],["Sureśvara","defends placing a \"mere grammar\" topic in an Upaniṣad — corrupt recitation corrupts meaning, and corrupt meaning blocks knowledge; thus Śīkṣā is aṅga (limb) to vidyā."],["Rangarāmānuja","correct svara especially matters because in Vedic usage a misplaced accent changes the deity/meaning; fidelity of sound is fidelity to the Lord's revealed word."],["Gambhīrānanda-tradition","notes the six as the standard list of the Prātiśākhya/Pāṇinīya Śikṣā; renders santāna as \"combination\" (the joining of letters), sāma as \"evenness/uniformity.\""],["Sri Aurobindo","sound is the first sheath of the Word; mastering the physics of utterance is the first step in mastering the mantra-śakti (power of the word) that later vallīs deploy."]],"notes":["Visual — the six limbs of Śīkṣā","<table><tr><td>#</td><td>Element</td><td>Governs</td><td>Example concern</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>varṇa</td><td>the phoneme</td><td>a vs. ā, ś vs. ṣ vs. s</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>svara</td><td>accent/pitch</td><td>udātta · anudātta · svarita</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>mātrā</td><td>duration</td><td>hrasva (1) · dīrgha (2) · pluta (3)</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>bala</td><td>effort/place of articulation</td><td>throat, palate, teeth, lips</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>sāma</td><td>evenness of tone</td><td>neither too fast nor uneven</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>santāna</td><td>continuity (sandhi)</td><td>unbroken euphonic flow</td></tr></table>","Practical steps / observations","•  Treat this as a checklist when chanting any mantra: is each phoneme (varṇa) distinct, each accent (svara) correct, each length (mātrā) honoured?","•  Bala (effort) and sthāna (place of articulation) are the somatic core — practise the five articulatory positions (guttural, palatal, retroflex, dental, labial) deliberately.","•  The placement of phonetics first in an Upaniṣad about Brahman is the teaching: the vehicle (speech) is disciplined before the cargo (knowledge) is loaded."]},{"ref":"Anuvāka 3","title":"Saṃhitā-upāsana: meditation on the junctions","sa":["सह नौ यशः । सह नौ ब्रह्मवर्चसम् ।","अथातः संहिताया उपनिषदं व्याख्यास्यामः ।","पञ्चस्वधिकरणेषु । अधिलोकमधिज्यौतिषमधिविद्यमधिप्रजमध्यात्मम् ।","ता महासंहिता इत्याचक्षते । अथाधिलोकम् ।","पृथिवी पूर्वरूपम् । द्यौरुत्तररूपम् । आकाशः सन्धिः ।","वायुः सन्धानम् । इत्यधिलोकम् । अथाधिज्यौतिषम् ।","अग्निः पूर्वरूपम् । आदित्य उत्तररूपम् । आपः सन्धिः ।","वैद्युतः सन्धानम् । इत्यधिज्यौतिषम् । अथाधिविद्यम् ।","आचार्यः पूर्वरूपम् । अन्तेवास्युत्तररूपम् । विद्या सन्धिः ।","प्रवचनं सन्धानम् । इत्यधिविद्यम् । अथाधिप्रजम् ।","माता पूर्वरूपम् । पितोत्तररूपम् । प्रजा सन्धिः ।","प्रजननं सन्धानम् । इत्यधिप्रजम् । अथाध्यात्मम् ।","अधराहनुः पूर्वरूपम् । उत्तराहनुरुत्तररूपम् । वाक्सन्धिः ।","जिह्वा सन्धानम् । इत्यध्यात्मम् । इतीमा महासंहिताः ।","य एवमेता महासंहिता व्याख्याता वेद ।","सन्धीयते प्रजया पशुभिः । ब्रह्मवर्चसेनान्नाद्येन सुवर्ग्येण लोकेन ॥"],"iast":["saha nau yaśaḥ | saha nau brahmavarcasam |","athātaḥ saṃhitāyā upaniṣadaṃ vyākhyāsyāmaḥ | pañcasv adhikaraṇeṣu |","adhilokam adhijyautiṣam adhividyam adhiprajam adhyātmam |","tā mahāsaṃhitā ity ācakṣate | athādhilokam |","pṛthivī pūrvarūpam | dyaur uttararūpam | ākāśaḥ sandhiḥ | vāyuḥ sandhānam |","... athādhyātmam | adharā hanuḥ pūrvarūpam | uttarā hanur uttararūpam |","vāk sandhiḥ | jihvā sandhānam | itīmā mahāsaṃhitāḥ |","ya evam etā mahāsaṃhitā vyākhyātā veda |","sandhīyate prajayā paśubhiḥ | brahmavarcasenānnādyena suvargyeṇa lokena ||"],"words":[["saṃhitāyāḥ upaniṣadam","f. gen. + f. acc.","the secret (meaning) of saṃhitā (junction/combination)"],["pañcasu adhikaraṇeṣu","n. loc. pl.","in five domains / loci"],["adhi-lokam","adv.","with reference to the worlds"],["adhi-jyautiṣam","adv.","with reference to the luminaries"],["adhi-vidyam","adv.","with reference to knowledge"],["adhi-prajam","adv.","with reference to progeny"],["adhi-ātmam","adv.","with reference to the body/self"],["pūrva-rūpam","n. nom. sg.","the prior form (first member)"],["uttara-rūpam","n. nom. sg.","the latter form (second member)"],["sandhiḥ","m. nom. sg.","the junction (meeting-point)"],["sandhānam","n. nom. sg.","the joiner / connecting medium"],["veda","3 sg. pf. (= pres.) of vid","(he who) knows"],["sandhīyate","3 sg. pres. pass.","is united / endowed"],["brahmavarcasena","n. instr. sg.","with spiritual lustre"],["anna-ādyena","n. instr. sg.","with food and the like"],["suvargyeṇa lokena","m. instr. sg.","with the heavenly world"]],"lit":"\"May glory be ours together; may spiritual lustre be ours together. Now, then, we shall expound the secret of saṃhitā (junction) in five domains: concerning the worlds, the luminaries, knowledge, progeny, and the self — these they call the Great Junctions… (in the self:) the lower jaw is the prior form, the upper jaw the latter form, speech the junction, the tongue the joiner. These are the Great Junctions. He who knows these Great Junctions thus expounded is united with progeny, cattle, spiritual lustre, food, and the heavenly world.\"","para":"Every saṃhitā (the \"joining\" that recitation literally performs between words) is meditated as a fourfold structure — prior form + junction + latter form + joiner — across five planes. The cosmic act of joining (earth↔heaven via space, joined by air; etc.) mirrors the act of joining sounds. Knowing this correspondence yields worldly and heavenly goods.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","this is upāsanā, not jñāna — a meditation yielding abhyudaya (prosperity), classed with karma. The fourfold pūrva/uttara/sandhi/sandhāna analysis of euphonic combination is projected onto five cosmic and bodily planes."],["Sureśvara","the result-clause proves the section is kāmya (desire-driven); he uses it to show that upāsanā and brahma-jñāna differ in fruit — one gives loka, the other mokṣa."],["Rangarāmānuja","the junctions display the Lord's ordering of reality; meditating on them is worship of His vibhūti, and the fruits flow from His grace, not mechanically."],["Gambhīrānanda-tradition","clarifies sandhi = the point of meeting, sandhāna = the means of joining; translates brahmavarcasa as \"Vedic/spiritual radiance,\" annādya as \"food and the means of eating.\""],["Sri Aurobindo","reads the obsessive pairing as the Vedic seer's habit of seeing every reality as a relation; the world is a fabric of conscious junctions, and to know the joinery is to gain its powers."]],"notes":["Visual — the five Great Junctions (mahā-saṃhitā)","<table><tr><td>Domain</td><td>Prior form (pūrva)</td><td>Junction (sandhi)</td><td>Latter form (uttara)</td><td>Joiner (sandhāna)</td></tr><tr><td>Worlds</td><td>earth</td><td>space (ākāśa)</td><td>heaven</td><td>air (vāyu)</td></tr><tr><td>Luminaries</td><td>fire</td><td>water (āpaḥ)</td><td>sun</td><td>lightning</td></tr><tr><td>Knowledge</td><td>teacher</td><td>knowledge (vidyā)</td><td>pupil</td><td>exposition (pravacana)</td></tr><tr><td>Progeny</td><td>mother</td><td>offspring (prajā)</td><td>father</td><td>procreation</td></tr><tr><td>Self/body</td><td>lower jaw</td><td>speech (vāk)</td><td>upper jaw</td><td>tongue</td></tr></table>","Practical steps / observations","•  Use the table as a contemplative grid: hold any pair (e.g. teacher–pupil) and dwell on what joins them and what stands between them — the meditation trains perception of relation, not just things.","•  The self-row (jaws–speech–tongue) brings the cosmic scheme into your own mouth as you chant — recitation itself becomes the adhyātma saṃhitā.","•  The stated fruits (progeny, cattle, lustre, food, heaven) mark this as sakāma upāsanā (meditation with desire); note how Vallī II will later drop all such fruits for mokṣa."]},{"ref":"Anuvāka 4","title":"Prayer for intelligence and students (the Āvahantī-homa)","sa":["यश्छन्दसामृषभो विश्वरूपः । छन्दोभ्योऽध्यमृतात्सम्बभूव ।","स मेन्द्रो मेधया स्पृणोतु । अमृतस्य देव धारणो भूयासम् ।","शरीरं मे विचर्षणम् । जिह्वा मे मधुमत्तमा । कर्णाभ्यां भूरिविश्रुवम् ।","ब्रह्मणः कोशोऽसि मेधया पिहितः । श्रुतं मे गोपाय ॥","आवहन्ती वितन्वाना । कुर्वाणाचीरमात्मनः ।","वासांसि मम गावश्च । अन्नपाने च सर्वदा ।","ततो मे श्रियमावह । लोमशां पशुभिः सह स्वाहा ।","आ मायन्तु ब्रह्मचारिणः स्वाहा । वि मायन्तु ब्रह्मचारिणः स्वाहा ।","प्र मायन्तु ब्रह्मचारिणः स्वाहा । दमायन्तु ब्रह्मचारिणः स्वाहा ।","शमायन्तु ब्रह्मचारिणः स्वाहा ॥","यशो जनेऽसानि स्वाहा । श्रेयान्वस्यसोऽसानि स्वाहा ।","तं त्वा भग प्रविशानि स्वाहा । स मा भग प्रविश स्वाहा ।","तस्मिन्सहस्रशाखे । निभगाहं त्वयि मृजे स्वाहा ।","यथापः प्रवता यन्ति । यथा मासा अहर्जरम् ।","एवं मां ब्रह्मचारिणः । धातरायन्तु सर्वतः स्वाहा ।","प्रतिवेशोऽसि प्र मा भाहि प्र मा पद्यस्व ॥"],"iast":["yaś chandasām ṛṣabho viśvarūpaḥ | chandobhyo 'dhy amṛtāt sambabhūva |","sa mendro medhayā spṛṇotu | amṛtasya deva dhāraṇo bhūyāsam |","śarīraṃ me vicarṣaṇam | jihvā me madhumattamā | karṇābhyāṃ bhūri viśruvam |","brahmaṇaḥ kośo 'si medhayā pihitaḥ | śrutaṃ me gopāya |","... ā māyantu brahmacāriṇaḥ svāhā | ... damāyantu ... śamāyantu brahmacāriṇaḥ svāhā |","... yathāpaḥ pravatā yanti | yathā māsā aharjaram | evaṃ māṃ brahmacāriṇo dhātar āyantu sarvataḥ svāhā |","prativeśo 'si pra mā bhāhi pra mā padyasva ||"],"words":[["chandasām ṛṣabhaḥ","n. gen. pl. + m. nom.","the bull (chief) of the metres/Vedas"],["viśva-rūpaḥ","m. nom. sg.","of all forms (i.e. Oṃ, the all-formed)"],["amṛtāt adhi sambabhūva","abl. + adv. + 3 sg. pf.","arose out of the immortal"],["medhayā","f. instr. sg.","with intelligence/retentive power"],["spṛṇotu","3 sg. impv. of spṛ","may (he) strengthen/grant"],["dhāraṇaḥ bhūyāsam","m. nom. sg. + 1 sg. benedictive","may I be a holder (of immortality)"],["vicarṣaṇam","n. nom. sg. adj.","active, capable"],["madhumat-tamā","f. nom. sg. superl.","most honey-sweet"],["brahmaṇaḥ kośaḥ asi","m. nom. + 2 sg.","you (Oṃ/intellect) are the sheath of Brahman"],["śrutam me gopāya","n. acc. + impv.","guard what I have heard"],["brahmacāriṇaḥ","m. nom. pl.","the celibate students"],["ā/vi/pra-māyantu","3 pl. impv.","may they come / spread out / come forth"],["dam-/śam-āyantu","3 pl. impv.","may they be self-controlled / tranquil"],["svāhā","indecl.","(oblation-word) \"well-offered!\""],["bhaga","m. voc. sg.","O Bhaga (Lord of fortune)"],["prativeśaḥ asi","m. nom. + 2 sg.","you are (my) refuge/neighbour"]],"lit":"\"That which is the bull of the metres, of all forms, which arose from the immortal above the metres — may that Indra strengthen me with intelligence. O God, may I be a holder of immortality. Let my body be vigorous, my tongue most sweet, let me hear abundantly with my ears. You are the sheath of Brahman, veiled by intellect — guard for me what I have learnt… May students come to me, spread to me, come forth to me; may they be self-controlled, may they be tranquil — svāhā… As waters flow downward, as months merge into the year, so, O Ordainer, may students come to me from all sides — svāhā. You are (my) refuge; illumine me, come to me.\"","para":"A teacher's homa (fire-offering) prayer in two movements: first for medhā (intelligence, retentive memory, sweet and far-reaching speech), then for students and prosperity to flow to him \"as rivers run downhill.\" Each wish is sealed with svāhā, marking it as an oblation. Oṃ (\"the bull of the metres, all-formed, sprung from the immortal\") is invoked as the seed of intelligence.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","a kāmya (desire-laden) japa-homa by one seeking medhā and disciples; \"bull of the metres, all-formed, born of the immortal\" is Oṃ (Praṇava), the essence distilled from the Vedas. The \"sheath of Brahman veiled by intellect\" is the buddhi that both reveals and screens Brahman."],["Sureśvara","even a desire for medhā is legitimate when its object is the retention of brahma-vidyā; the prayer is vidyāṅga (a limb supporting knowledge), not mere worldly ambition."],["Rangarāmānuja","\"that Indra\" and \"Bhaga\" are forms of the supreme Lord who alone grants intelligence and fortune; the inflow of students is His arrangement, sought through prapatti-like surrender (\"illumine me, come to me\")."],["Gambhīrānanda-tradition","settles chandasām ṛṣabhaḥ … viśvarūpaḥ as epithets of Oṃ; renders brahmaṇaḥ kośaḥ … medhayā pihitaḥ as \"you are the receptacle of Brahman, covered (veiled) by intelligence.\""],["Sri Aurobindo","reads medhā as the seizing-power of the illumined intellect and the prayer for students as the natural radiation of a realised teacher — śrī (prosperity) being the outer sign of inner plenitude."]],"notes":["Visual — the two prayers of Anuvāka 4","<table><tr><td>Movement</td><td>Asks for</td><td>Imagery</td><td>Seal</td></tr><tr><td>A. Medhā (intellect)</td><td>retentive memory, vigorous body, sweet tongue, keen ears, guarding of learning</td><td>Oṃ as \"bull of the metres,\" the sheath of Brahman</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>B. Śrī + śiṣyas (prosperity &amp; pupils)</td><td>students arriving, self-controlled &amp; calm; wealth, food, cattle</td><td>\"as rivers run downhill, as months merge into the year\"</td><td>svāhā (oblation)</td></tr></table>","Practical steps / observations","•  This is a working upāsanā for any teacher or learner: invoke Oṃ as the source of memory before study; ask not just for intellect but for a sweet tongue and keen hearing — the whole receptive apparatus.","•  Note the ethical conditioning: the students prayed for are dama- and śama-yukta (self-controlled and tranquil) — the prayer is for worthy pupils, not merely many.","•  The downhill-river image is a meditation on effortless inflow: prosperity and students come by natural law when the teacher is fit, not by grasping."]},{"ref":"Anuvāka 5","title":"The Vyāhṛtis: Bhūr Bhuvaḥ Suvaḥ Mahaḥ","sa":["भूर्भुवः सुवरिति वा एतास्तिस्रो व्याहृतयः ।","तासामु ह स्मैतां चतुर्थीम् । माहाचमस्यः प्रवेदयते ।","मह इति । तद्ब्रह्म । स आत्मा । अङ्गान्यन्या देवताः ।","भूरिति वा अयं लोकः । भुव इत्यन्तरिक्षम् । सुवरित्यसौ लोकः ॥","मह इत्यादित्यः । आदित्येन वाव सर्वे लोका महीयन्ते ।","भूरिति वा अग्निः । भुव इति वायुः । सुवरित्यादित्यः ।","मह इति चन्द्रमाः । चन्द्रमसा वाव सर्वाणि ज्योतींषि महीयन्ते ।","भूरिति वा ऋचः । भुव इति सामानि । सुवरिति यजूंषि ॥","मह इति ब्रह्म । ब्रह्मणा वाव सर्वे वेदा महीयन्ते ।","भूरिति वै प्राणः । भुव इत्यपानः । सुवरिति व्यानः ।","मह इत्यन्नम् । अन्नेन वाव सर्वे प्राणा महीयन्ते ।","ता वा एताश्चतस्रश्चतुर्धा । चतस्रश्चतस्रो व्याहृतयः ।","ता यो वेद । स वेद ब्रह्म । सर्वेऽस्मै देवा बलिमावहन्ति ॥"],"iast":["bhūr bhuvaḥ suvar iti vā etās tisro vyāhṛtayaḥ |","tāsām u ha smaitāṃ caturthīm | māhācamasyaḥ pravedayate |","maha iti | tad brahma | sa ātmā | aṅgāny anyā devatāḥ |","bhūr iti vā ayaṃ lokaḥ | bhuva ity antarikṣam | suvar ity asau lokaḥ |","maha ity ādityaḥ | ... maha iti candramāḥ | ... maha iti brahma | ... maha ity annam |","tā vā etāś catasraś caturdhā | catasraś catasro vyāhṛtayaḥ |","tā yo veda | sa veda brahma | sarve 'smai devā balim āvahanti ||"],"words":[["bhūḥ, bhuvaḥ, suvaḥ","indecl.","the three \"utterances\" (earth, mid-region, heaven)"],["vyāhṛtayaḥ","f. nom. pl.","the vyāhṛtis (sacred utterances)"],["caturthīm","f. acc. sg.","the fourth (one)"],["māhācamasyaḥ","m. nom. sg.","(the sage) son of Mahācamasa"],["pravedayate","3 sg. pres. mid. caus.","makes known / teaches"],["mahaḥ","indecl.","\"Mahaḥ,\" the great (fourth utterance)"],["tat brahma · sa ātmā","n. + m. nom.","that is Brahman; it is the Self"],["aṅgāni anyāḥ devatāḥ","n. nom. pl. + f.","the other deities are (its) limbs"],["ayam lokaḥ","m. nom. sg.","this world (earth)"],["antarikṣam","n. nom. sg.","the mid-region"],["asau lokaḥ","m. nom. sg.","yonder world (heaven)"],["mahīyante","3 pl. pres. pass.","are magnified / nourished"],["balim āvahanti","m. acc. + 3 pl.","bring tribute/offerings"]],"lit":"\"Bhūḥ, Bhuvaḥ, Suvaḥ — these indeed are the three utterances. Of them the son of Mahācamasa made known a fourth: Mahaḥ. That is Brahman; that is the Self; the other deities are its limbs… Bhūḥ is this world, Bhuvaḥ the mid-region, Suvaḥ yonder world, Mahaḥ the sun — by the sun all worlds are magnified… These four are fourfold; (so) the four utterances are (each) four. He who knows them knows Brahman; to him all the gods bring tribute.\"","para":"The three vyāhṛtis (the formula prefixed to the Gāyatrī) gain a fourth, Mahaḥ, which is Brahman/Self — the others its limbs. Each utterance is then meditated across four registers (worlds, luminaries, Vedas, breaths), with Mahaḥ always the unifying surplus (sun, moon, Brahman, food). To know this fourfold-fourfold scheme is to \"know Brahman,\" and the gods themselves serve the knower.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","a samaṣṭi-vyaṣṭi (collective–individual) upāsanā; Mahaḥ = Brahman conditioned as the all-magnifying principle. The result (\"knows Brahman\") is figurative — it means knowing the saguṇa Brahman of this meditation, not the unconditioned of Vallī II."],["Sureśvara","the \"fourth utterance revealed by Māhācamasya\" shows the upāsanā is a sage's contemplative discovery, layered onto the ritual vyāhṛtis; he carefully ranks this saguṇa fruit below liberation."],["Rangarāmānuja","Mahaḥ as Brahman is the supreme Person of whom sun, moon, Vedas and food are vibhūtis; \"the gods bring tribute to the knower\" because the Lord exalts His devotee."],["Gambhīrānanda-tradition","identifies Māhācamasya as a ṛṣi (son of Mahācamasa); stresses the distributive sense of catasraś caturdhā — each of the four utterances is itself meditated in four ways."],["Sri Aurobindo","the vyāhṛtis are the planes of the manifest being — physical (Bhūḥ), vital (Bhuvaḥ), mental-heaven (Suvaḥ) — crowned by Mahaḥ, the great causal Truth-plane (his Mahas / vijñāna) from which the others derive."]],"notes":["Visual — the fourfold Vyāhṛti grid","<table><tr><td>Register →</td><td>Bhūḥ</td><td>Bhuvaḥ</td><td>Suvaḥ</td><td>Mahaḥ (the unifier)</td></tr><tr><td>Worlds</td><td>earth</td><td>mid-region</td><td>heaven</td><td>the sun (magnifies all worlds)</td></tr><tr><td>Luminaries</td><td>fire</td><td>air</td><td>sun</td><td>the moon (magnifies all lights)</td></tr><tr><td>Vedas</td><td>Ṛc</td><td>Sāman</td><td>Yajus</td><td>Brahman (magnifies all Vedas)</td></tr><tr><td>Breaths</td><td>prāṇa</td><td>apāna</td><td>vyāna</td><td>food (annam) (magnifies all breaths)</td></tr></table>","Practical steps / observations","•  Chant bhūr bhuvaḥ suvaḥ (the Gāyatrī's śiras) with this grid in mind: each syllable opens onto a whole plane of reality, and Mahaḥ gathers them — a built-in expansion-and-integration meditation.","•  The recurring word is mahīyante (\"are magnified\"): contemplate how in each register one principle (sun/moon/Brahman/food) nourishes and exalts the rest — train the eye for the integrating factor.","•  \"Food magnifies all breaths\" quietly seeds Vallī III's anna-vidyā — note the foreshadowing."]},{"ref":"Anuvāka 6","title":"The Puruṣa in the heart-ākāśa and the upward path","sa":["स य एषोऽन्तर्हृदय आकाशः । तस्मिन्नयं पुरुषो मनोमयः ।","अमृतो हिरण्मयः । अन्तरेण तालुके । य एष स्तन इवावलम्बते ।","सेन्द्रयोनिः । यत्रासौ केशान्तो विवर्तते । व्यपोह्य शीर्षकपाले ।","भूरित्यग्नौ प्रतितिष्ठति । भुव इति वायौ ॥","सुवरित्यादित्ये । मह इति ब्रह्मणि । आप्नोति स्वाराज्यम् ।","आप्नोति मनसस्पतिम् । वाक्पतिश्चक्षुष्पतिः । श्रोत्रपतिर्विज्ञानपतिः ।","एतत्ततो भवति । आकाशशरीरं ब्रह्म । सत्यात्म प्राणारामं मन आनन्दम् ।","शान्तिसमृद्धममृतम् । इति प्राचीनयोग्योपास्व ॥"],"iast":["sa ya eṣo 'ntar-hṛdaya ākāśaḥ | tasminn ayaṃ puruṣo manomayaḥ |","amṛto hiraṇmayaḥ | antareṇa tāluke | ya eṣa stana ivāvalambate |","sendrayoniḥ | yatrāsau keśānto vivartate | vyapohya śīrṣakapāle |","bhūr ity agnau pratitiṣṭhati | bhuva iti vāyau | suvar ity āditye | maha iti brahmaṇi |","āpnoti svārājyam | āpnoti manasaspatim | vākpatiś cakṣuṣpatiḥ | śrotrapatir vijñānapatiḥ |","etat tato bhavati | ākāśaśarīraṃ brahma | satyātma prāṇārāmaṃ mana ānandam |","śāntisamṛddham amṛtam | iti prācīnayogyopāssva ||"],"words":[["antar-hṛdaye ākāśaḥ","m. loc. + nom.","the space within the heart"],["puruṣaḥ manomayaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the Person made of mind"],["amṛtaḥ hiraṇmayaḥ","m. nom. sg.","immortal, golden (luminous)"],["antareṇa tāluke","adv. + n. loc. du.","between the two palates"],["stanaḥ iva avalambate","m. nom. + 3 sg.","hangs down like a nipple (the uvula)"],["sā indra-yoniḥ","f. nom. sg.","that is the source/womb of Indra (the Self)"],["keśa-antaḥ vivartate","m. nom. + 3 sg.","where the hair-end parts (the crown)"],["vyapohya śīrṣa-kapāle","ger. + n. loc.","having parted the skull-halves"],["pratitiṣṭhati","3 sg. pres.","is established / takes one's stand"],["svārājyam","n. acc. sg.","self-sovereignty, autonomy"],["manasaḥ patim","m. acc. sg.","the lord of the mind"],["ākāśa-śarīram brahma","n. nom. sg.","Brahman whose body is space"],["satya-ātma","n.","whose nature is truth"],["prāṇa-ārāmam","n.","whose delight is life-breath"],["mana-ānandam","n.","whose bliss is the mind"],["śānti-samṛddham amṛtam","n.","rich in peace, immortal"],["prācīna-yogya upāssva","m. voc. + 2 sg. impv.","\"O Prācīnayogya, meditate (thus)!\""]],"lit":"\"There is this space within the heart; in it is the Person made of mind, immortal and golden. (The channel) between the two palates — that which hangs down like a nipple (the uvula) — is the womb of Indra (the way to the Self), there where the roots of the hair part, parting the halves of the skull. (Uttering) Bhūḥ one is established in fire; Bhuvaḥ, in air; Suvaḥ, in the sun; Mahaḥ, in Brahman. He attains self-sovereignty; he attains the lord of the mind; he becomes lord of speech, of sight, of hearing, of understanding. And then he becomes this: Brahman whose body is space, whose self is truth, whose delight is breath, whose bliss is mind, replete with peace, immortal. Thus, O Prācīnayogya, meditate.\"","para":"Brahman is located in the heart-ākāśa as a luminous \"mind-made Person.\" A subtle path is described — through the uvula and the suture of the skull (brahma-randhra) — up which the knower rises, stepping through the four vyāhṛti-planes (fire→air→sun→Brahman) to reach svārājya (self-rule) and lordship over all the faculties, finally becoming Brahman defined by the chain satya–prāṇa–mana–ānanda–śānti–amṛta.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the \"mind-made Person\" is saguṇa Brahman for upāsanā; the path through the suṣumnā and brahma-randhra is the uttarāyaṇa / devayāna route of the krama-mukta (gradually liberated) worshipper who reaches Brahmaloka, not the immediate jīvan-mukti of the nirguṇa knower."],["Sureśvara","insists the localisation \"in the heart\" is for the meditator's convenience; the unconditioned Self is not literally spatial, but the upāsaka needs a pratīka and a gati (route)."],["Rangarāmānuja","this is the dahara-vidyā of Nārāyaṇa dwelling in the heart-lotus; the upward path is the soul's arcirādi-gati to Vaikuṇṭha, and \"self-sovereignty\" is the released soul's likeness to the Lord — not identity with Him."],["Gambhīrānanda-tradition","carefully maps the cryptic anatomy — antareṇa tāluke (between the palates), the uvula (stana-iva), keśānta (crown) — and names Prācīnayogya as the pupil being addressed."],["Sri Aurobindo","the heart-ākāśa is the secret seat of the psychic being; the ascent through Bhūr–Bhuvaḥ–Suvaḥ–Mahas is the rising of consciousness through physical, vital, mental and supramental planes to its kingship (svārājya) over the instruments."]],"notes":["Visual — the heart-Person and the upward ascent","<pre>        crown / brahma-randhra  ─── &quot;where the hair-end parts&quot;</pre>","▲   (skull-halves parted)","│","<pre>            uvula (stana-like) = indra-yoni  ── path between the palates</pre>","▲","<pre>          ┌──────┴──────┐</pre>","<pre>          │  HEART-ĀKĀŚA │  ← Puruṣa: mano-maya · amṛta · hiraṇmaya</pre>","<pre>          └─────────────┘</pre>","Ascent through the vyāhṛti-planes:","Bhūḥ → fire | Bhuvaḥ → air | Suvaḥ → sun | Mahaḥ → Brahman","↓ yields","svārājya (self-rule) · lord of mind/speech/eye/ear/understanding","↓ becomes","Brahman = ākāśa-body · satya-self · prāṇa-delight · mana-bliss · śānti · amṛta","Practical steps / observations","•  This is a dahara-vidyā–type meditation: locate awareness in the heart-space, hold the \"luminous mind-Person\" there, and let attention rise along the central axis with the vyāhṛtis.","•  The named nāḍī through the uvula and crown is the classical suṣumnā / brahma-randhra route — treat the anatomy as a contemplative map, not a claim to be forced physically.","•  The closing chain (satya → prāṇa → manas → ānanda → śānti → amṛta) is a portable definition of Brahman to rest the mind on; it also previews the ānanda of Vallī II."]},{"ref":"Anuvāka 7","title":"Pāṅkta: everything is fivefold","sa":["पृथिव्यन्तरिक्षं द्यौर्दिशोऽवान्तरदिशाः ।","अग्निर्वायुरादित्यश्चन्द्रमा नक्षत्राणि ।","आप ओषधयो वनस्पतय आकाश आत्मा । इत्यधिभूतम् ।","अथाध्यात्मम् । प्राणो व्यानोऽपान उदानः समानः ।","चक्षुः श्रोत्रं मनो वाक् त्वक् । चर्ममांसं स्नावास्थि मज्जा ।","एतदधिविधाय ऋषिरवोचत् । पाङ्क्तं वा इदं सर्वम् ।","पाङ्क्तेनैव पाङ्क्तं स्पृणोतीति ॥"],"iast":["pṛthivy antarikṣaṃ dyaur diśo 'vāntaradiśāḥ |","agnir vāyur ādityaś candramā nakṣatrāṇi |","āpa oṣadhayo vanaspataya ākāśa ātmā | ity adhibhūtam |","athādhyātmam | prāṇo vyāno 'pāna udānaḥ samānaḥ |","cakṣuḥ śrotraṃ mano vāk tvak | carma māṃsaṃ snāvāsthi majjā |","etad adhividhāya ṛṣir avocat | pāṅktaṃ vā idaṃ sarvam |","pāṅktenaiva pāṅktaṃ spṛṇotīti ||"],"words":[["diśaḥ avāntara-diśāḥ","f. nom. pl.","the quarters and intermediate quarters"],["adhibhūtam","n. nom. sg.","with reference to the elements (cosmic)"],["adhyātmam","n. nom. sg.","with reference to the self (bodily)"],["prāṇa, apāna, vyāna, udāna, samāna","m. nom. sg.","the five vital airs"],["cakṣuḥ, śrotram, manaḥ, vāk, tvak","n./f. nom.","eye, ear, mind, speech, skin"],["carma, māṃsa, snāva, asthi, majjā","n./m./f. nom.","skin, flesh, sinew, bone, marrow"],["adhividhāya","ger. of adhi-vi-dhā","having arranged/correlated"],["ṛṣiḥ avocat","m. nom. + 3 sg. impf.","the seer declared"],["pāṅktam","n. nom. sg. adj.","fivefold (of five)"],["spṛṇoti","3 sg. pres.","wins / strengthens / completes"]],"lit":"\"Earth, mid-region, heaven, the quarters, the intermediate quarters; fire, air, sun, moon, stars; water, herbs, trees, space, the body — this is (the fivefold) with reference to the elements. Now with reference to the self: prāṇa, vyāna, apāna, udāna, samāna; eye, ear, mind, speech, touch; skin, flesh, sinew, bone, marrow. Having so arranged it, the seer declared: 'All this is fivefold; by the fivefold one wins the fivefold.'\"","para":"Reality is read as a series of pentads — three cosmic sets of five (locations, luminaries, substances) and three bodily sets of five (breaths, senses, tissues). Because the macrocosm and microcosm share the same fivefold structure, \"by the fivefold one secures the fivefold\": the yajña (also fivefold) and the meditation on fives knit cosmos and self together.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","a sampad-upāsanā (meditation by numerical correspondence); the fivefold prajāpati / fivefold sacrifice is reached because the worshipper's own constitution is fivefold. The fruit is identification with the cosmic fivefold, a saguṇa attainment."],["Sureśvara","the \"seer's declaration\" gives scriptural warrant; he notes such number-meditations train the mind in seeing unity-in-multiplicity, a rung below the non-dual seeing of Vallī II."],["Rangarāmānuja","the pentads are the Lord's ordered vibhūti; cosmos and body share His design, and meditating the homology is worship of His all-pervading rule."],["Gambhīrānanda-tradition","clarifies adhividhāya as \"having superimposed/correlated\" and spṛṇoti as \"wins/strengthens\"; lists the pentads in the text's exact order."],["Sri Aurobindo","the recurrence of five expresses the Veda's perception of a single creative rhythm structuring both world and organism; the meditation is a discipline of seeing the cosmos in the body."]],"notes":["Visual — the six pentads","<table><tr><td>Plane</td><td>The five</td></tr><tr><td>Adhibhūta · regions</td><td>earth · mid-region · heaven · quarters · sub-quarters</td></tr><tr><td>Adhibhūta · luminaries</td><td>fire · air · sun · moon · stars</td></tr><tr><td>Adhibhūta · substances</td><td>water · herbs · trees · space · the body</td></tr><tr><td>Adhyātma · breaths</td><td>prāṇa · vyāna · apāna · udāna · samāna</td></tr><tr><td>Adhyātma · senses</td><td>eye · ear · mind · speech · touch</td></tr><tr><td>Adhyātma · tissues</td><td>skin · flesh · sinew · bone · marrow</td></tr></table>","Practical steps / observations","•  Use the six pentads as a structured body-and-world scan: name each set of five slowly, feeling the correspondence between an outer pentad and its inner mirror.","•  The teaching is homology: train yourself to see the same pattern (five) repeating across scales — the antidote to seeing self and world as unrelated.","•  \"By the fivefold one wins the fivefold\" frames ritual and meditation as pattern-matching — you reach a structure by enacting its likeness."]},{"ref":"Anuvāka 8","title":"Oṃ: the syllable that is Brahman","sa":["ओमिति ब्रह्म । ओमितीदं सर्वम् ।","ओमित्येतदनुकृतिर्ह स्म वा अप्योश्रावयेत्याश्रावयन्ति ।","ओमिति सामानि गायन्ति । ॐशोमिति शस्त्राणि शंसन्ति ।","ओमित्यध्वर्युः प्रतिगरं प्रतिगृणाति । ओमिति ब्रह्मा प्रसौति ।","ओमित्यग्निहोत्रमनुजानाति । ओमिति ब्राह्मणः प्रवक्ष्यन्नाह ब्रह्मोपाप्नवानीति ।","ब्रह्मैवोपाप्नोति ॥"],"iast":["om iti brahma | om itīdaṃ sarvam |","om ity etad anukṛtir ha sma vā apy o-śrāvayety āśrāvayanti |","om iti sāmāni gāyanti | oṃ śom iti śastrāṇi śaṃsanti |","om ity adhvaryuḥ pratigaraṃ pratigṛṇāti | om iti brahmā prasauti |","om ity agnihotram anujānāti | om iti brāhmaṇaḥ pravakṣyann āha brahmopāpnavānīti |","brahmaivopāpnoti ||"],"words":[["om iti brahma","indecl. + n.","\"Oṃ\" — (this is) Brahman"],["idam sarvam","n. nom. sg.","all this (universe)"],["anukṛtiḥ","f. nom. sg.","(sign of) assent / \"so be it,\" imitation"],["o-śrāvaya · āśrāvayanti","impv. + 3 pl. pres.","\"cause to hear!\" — they make heard (the call to attention)"],["sāmāni gāyanti","n. acc. pl. + 3 pl.","they sing the Sāman chants"],["śastrāṇi śaṃsanti","n. acc. pl. + 3 pl.","they recite the Śastra praises"],["adhvaryuḥ pratigaram pratigṛṇāti","m. nom. + 3 sg.","the Adhvaryu utters the response"],["brahmā prasauti","m. nom. + 3 sg.","the Brahman-priest gives assent to begin"],["agnihotram anujānāti","n. acc. + 3 sg.","sanctions the fire-oblation"],["brāhmaṇaḥ pravakṣyan āha","m. nom. + fut.part. + 3 sg.","the brahmin about to recite says"],["brahma upāpnavāni","n. + 1 sg. subj.","\"may I attain Brahman\""],["brahma eva upāpnoti","n. + 3 sg.","he attains Brahman alone"]],"lit":"\"'Oṃ' — (is) Brahman. 'Oṃ' — (is) all this. With 'Oṃ' (one signals) assent; and indeed, saying 'O-śrāvaya' they make (the gods) heard. With 'Oṃ' they sing the Sāmans; with 'Oṃ Śom' they recite the Śastras; with 'Oṃ' the Adhvaryu utters his response; with 'Oṃ' the Brahman-priest gives the start; with 'Oṃ' one sanctions the Agnihotra; with 'Oṃ' the brahmin about to recite says, 'May I attain Brahman' — and he attains Brahman indeed.\"","para":"Oṃ is declared to be Brahman and the whole universe, and is shown to pervade every ritual act as the sound of assent and commencement. To utter Oṃ at the threshold of recitation, resolving \"may I reach Brahman,\" is itself to reach Brahman — sound and reality coincide in the Praṇava.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","Oṃ is both vācaka (the word) and, by the closest pratīka, identical with the vācya (Brahman). Uttered as assent it carries the whole sacrifice; meditated as Brahman it yields Brahman. He links this to Praṇava-upāsanā (cf. Māṇḍūkya)."],["Sureśvara","analyses how a mere sound can \"be\" Brahman — by the relation of symbol and signified; the upāsaka who fixes on Oṃ as Brahman gradually transfers the reality of the signified onto his cognition."],["Rangarāmānuja","Oṃ is the Lord's own name and the seed of all the Vedas; uttered in surrender it secures Him. \"Attains Brahman\" = attains the supreme Person whom Oṃ denotes."],["Gambhīrānanda-tradition","glosses the ritual technicalities — āśrāvaṇa, pratigara, praṇava in Sāman vs. Śastra (oṃ vs. oṃ śom) — so the reader sees Oṃ literally threading every priestly office."],["Sri Aurobindo","Oṃ is the sound-body of the Eternal, the seed-vibration from which the worlds proceed; to intone it with the will toward Brahman is to ride the original creative sound back to its source."]],"notes":["Visual — Oṃ across the ritual field","<table><tr><td>Where Oṃ sounds</td><td>Who/what</td><td>Role of Oṃ</td></tr><tr><td>om iti brahma / sarvam</td><td>metaphysics</td><td>Oṃ = Brahman = all</td></tr><tr><td>āśrāvayanti</td><td>call to attention</td><td>summons the act</td></tr><tr><td>sāma-gāna</td><td>the Udgātṛ</td><td>opens the chant</td></tr><tr><td>śastra-śaṃsana (oṃ śom)</td><td>the Hotṛ</td><td>opens the recitation</td></tr><tr><td>pratigara</td><td>the Adhvaryu</td><td>the ritual response</td></tr><tr><td>prasava</td><td>the Brahman-priest</td><td>sanction to begin</td></tr><tr><td>agnihotra-anujñā</td><td>daily oblation</td><td>permission/assent</td></tr><tr><td>brāhmaṇaḥ pravakṣyan</td><td>the reciter's resolve</td><td>\"may I attain Brahman\" → attains it</td></tr></table>","Practical steps / observations","•  Begin every recitation, study, or meditation with Oṃ held as both \"yes\" (assent to what is) and \"let it begin\" (commencement) — the anuvāka's two functions of the syllable.","•  Pair the utterance with the resolve brahma upāpnavāni (\"may I reach Brahman\"); the text's promise is that intention voiced in Oṃ is already arrival.","•  Observe how Oṃ is the one constant across many different ritual roles — a meditation on the single sound underlying all sanctioned action."]},{"ref":"Anuvāka 9","title":"Svādhyāya: the praise of study, and three teachers' views","sa":["ऋतं च स्वाध्यायप्रवचने च । सत्यं च स्वाध्यायप्रवचने च ।","तपश्च स्वाध्यायप्रवचने च । दमश्च स्वाध्यायप्रवचने च ।","शमश्च स्वाध्यायप्रवचने च । अग्नयश्च स्वाध्यायप्रवचने च ।","अग्निहोत्रं च स्वाध्यायप्रवचने च । अतिथयश्च स्वाध्यायप्रवचने च ।","मानुषं च स्वाध्यायप्रवचने च । प्रजा च स्वाध्यायप्रवचने च ।","प्रजनश्च स्वाध्यायप्रवचने च । प्रजातिश्च स्वाध्यायप्रवचने च ।","सत्यमिति सत्यवचा राथीतरः । तप इति तपोनित्यः पौरुशिष्टिः ।","स्वाध्यायप्रवचने एवेति नाको मौद्गल्यः । तद्धि तपस्तद्धि तपः ॥"],"iast":["ṛtaṃ ca svādhyāya-pravacane ca | satyaṃ ca svādhyāya-pravacane ca |","tapaś ca ... | damaś ca ... | śamaś ca ... | agnayaś ca ... | agnihotraṃ ca ... |","atithayaś ca ... | mānuṣaṃ ca ... | prajā ca ... | prajanaś ca ... | prajātiś ca svādhyāya-pravacane ca |","satyam iti satyavacā rāthītaraḥ | tapa iti taponityaḥ pauruśiṣṭiḥ |","svādhyāya-pravacane eveti nāko maudgalyaḥ | taddhi tapas taddhi tapaḥ ||"],"words":[["ṛtam","n. nom. sg.","right (truth as cosmic/mental order)"],["svādhyāya-pravacane","n. nom. du.","self-study (recitation) and teaching"],["satyam","n. nom. sg.","truth (in speech/conduct)"],["tapaḥ","n. nom. sg.","austerity"],["damaḥ · śamaḥ","m. nom. sg.","outer control (senses) · inner control (mind)"],["agnayaḥ","m. nom. pl.","the (three sacred) fires"],["agnihotram","n. nom. sg.","the daily fire-oblation"],["atithayaḥ","m. nom. pl.","guests (hospitality)"],["mānuṣam","n. nom. sg.","(duties) human/social"],["prajā · prajanaḥ · prajātiḥ","f./m./f. nom.","offspring · procreation · grandchildren (continuance)"],["satyavacā rāthītaraḥ","m. nom. sg.","Satyavacas of the Rāthītara line"],["taponityaḥ pauruśiṣṭiḥ","m. nom. sg.","Taponitya of the Pauruśiṣṭi line"],["nākaḥ maudgalyaḥ","m. nom. sg.","Nāka of the Maudgalya line"],["tat hi tapaḥ","pron. + indecl. + n.","\"that indeed is austerity\""]],"lit":"\"Right, and self-study and teaching; truth, and self-study and teaching; austerity… self-control (outer)… self-control (inner)… the fires… the Agnihotra… guests… human duty… offspring… procreation… progeny — (all these are to be practised) together with self-study and teaching. 'Truth (above all),' says Satyavacas Rāthītara. 'Austerity,' says Taponitya Pauruśiṣṭi. 'Self-study and teaching alone,' says Nāka Maudgalya — for that indeed is austerity, that indeed is austerity.\"","para":"A litany pairing every duty — ethical (ṛta, satya), ascetic (tapas, dama, śama), ritual (agni, agnihotra), social (atithi, mānuṣa), and generative (prajā, prajana, prajāti) — with the constant refrain svādhyāya-pravacana (\"study and teaching\"). Three sages then weigh in on what is supreme: truth (Satyavacas), austerity (Taponitya), or study-and-teaching itself (Nāka) — the text endorsing the last by its emphatic repetition.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the section enjoins not abandoning the duties, but performing them along with study and teaching; the three opinions are arthavāda (eulogy) magnifying each practice, with Nāka's view stamped as final because study/teaching sustains all the rest."],["Sureśvara","harmonises the three: satya and tapas are included in svādhyāya-pravacana rightly done; the repetition is the Upaniṣad's siddhānta that scriptural study-and-transmission is the master-discipline."],["Rangarāmānuja","all these are kaiṅkarya (loving service) ordained by the Lord; study of His revelation crowns them because it is the means by which all other duties are known and offered."],["Gambhīrānanda-tradition","fixes svādhyāya = one's own recitation/study of the Veda, pravacana = teaching it to others; renders the named sages with their patronymics and notes the emphatic hi … hi."],["Sri Aurobindo","svādhyāya is the self's study of the Word that remakes it; placing study-and-teaching above even truth and austerity marks the centrality of the illumined Word in the discipline of self-exceeding."]],"notes":["Visual — duties × the constant, and the three verdicts","<table><tr><td>Cluster</td><td>Duties named</td><td>Always paired with</td></tr><tr><td>Ethical</td><td>ṛta, satya</td><td>svādhyāya + pravacana</td></tr><tr><td>Ascetic</td><td>tapas, dama, śama</td><td>svādhyāya + pravacana</td></tr><tr><td>Ritual</td><td>the fires, agnihotra</td><td>svādhyāya + pravacana</td></tr><tr><td>Social</td><td>guests, human duty</td><td>svādhyāya + pravacana</td></tr><tr><td>Generative</td><td>offspring, procreation, progeny</td><td>svādhyāya + pravacana</td></tr></table>","<table><tr><td>Sage</td><td>Supreme practice</td></tr><tr><td>Satyavacas Rāthītara</td><td>Truth (satya)</td></tr><tr><td>Taponitya Pauruśiṣṭi</td><td>Austerity (tapas)</td></tr><tr><td>Nāka Maudgalya</td><td>Study &amp; teaching — \"that is the (real) austerity\" (endorsed)</td></tr></table>","Practical steps / observations","•  Read the refrain as a rule of life: whatever else you do — ethics, ritual, family, hospitality — keep studying and keep teaching; transmission is non-negotiable.","•  The three verdicts are not contradictions but a hierarchy of temperament; the text's doubled close (\"taddhi tapaḥ\") quietly elevates svādhyāya-pravacana as the austerity that contains the others.","•  Observe that teaching (pravacana) is yoked to study (svādhyāya) at every step — you are asked to give back what you take in."]},{"ref":"Anuvāka 10","title":"Triśaṅku's proclamation of realisation","sa":["अहं वृक्षस्य रेरिवा । कीर्तिः पृष्ठं गिरेरिव ।","ऊर्ध्वपवित्रो वाजिनीव स्वमृतमस्मि ।","द्रविणं सवर्चसम् । सुमेधा अमृतोक्षितः ।","इति त्रिशङ्कोर्वेदानुवचनम् ॥"],"iast":["ahaṃ vṛkṣasya rerivā | kīrtiḥ pṛṣṭhaṃ girer iva |","ūrdhva-pavitro vājinīva svamṛtam asmi |","draviṇaṃ savarcasam | sumedhā amṛtokṣitaḥ |","iti triśaṅkor vedānuvacanam ||"],"words":[["aham","pron. nom. sg.","I"],["vṛkṣasya rerivā","m. gen. + m. nom.","the mover/impeller of the tree (of the world)"],["kīrtiḥ","f. nom. sg.","(my) fame/glory"],["pṛṣṭham girer iva","n. nom. + adv.","(high) like the back/peak of a mountain"],["ūrdhva-pavitraḥ","m. nom. sg.","purified-on-high / whose source is pure & lofty"],["vājinī iva","f. nom. + adv.","like (the light/essence) in the sun (\"vājinī\")"],["su-amṛtam asmi","n. + 1 sg.","I am the pure immortal (Self)"],["draviṇam savarcasam","n. nom. sg.","(I am) wealth, radiant"],["su-medhāḥ","m. nom. sg.","of fine intelligence"],["amṛta-ukṣitaḥ","m. nom. sg. pp.","sprinkled with / made immortal"],["triśaṅkoḥ veda-anuvacanam","m. gen. + n.","the Veda-recitation (realisation-hymn) of Triśaṅku"]],"lit":"\"I am the impeller of the tree (of the world). My glory is lofty like a mountain peak. Pure-on-high, like the radiance in the sun, I am the pure immortal. I am radiant wealth; I am of fine intelligence, immortal, undecaying. — This is Triśaṅku's Veda-proclamation (on attaining knowledge).\"","para":"A ṛṣi named Triśaṅku, on realisation, bursts into a first-person declaration of identity with the Self: he is the mover of the world-tree (its very root-impulse), lofty as a peak, pure, luminous, the deathless essence, intelligence itself. It is a brahma-jñāna-prakāśaka mantra — a song of the realised \"I.\"","coms":[["Śaṅkara","Triśaṅku's hymn is aham-graha-upāsanā / a realiser's anubhava-vāṇī; \"mover of the tree\" = the Self that sets the saṃsāra-vṛkṣa in motion. The whole is the spontaneous self-expression of brahma-bhāva."],["Sureśvara","such first-person declarations are warranted only after tattva-jñāna; he uses the verse to show that the jñānin's \"I\" is the universal Self, not the ego."],["Rangarāmānuja","reads the exalted \"I\" as the liberated soul glorifying its attained likeness to Brahman and its role within the Lord's cosmic tree — not an assertion of absolute identity."],["Gambhīrānanda-tradition","notes the verse's archaic, riddling diction (rerivā, vājinī) and the range of construals; settles on the realiser's self-celebration as the through-line."],["Sri Aurobindo","a Vedic mantra of self-discovery — the soul recognising itself as the dynamic root of the cosmic tree and as the immortal light; the \"I\" here is the evolved, self-aware spirit."]],"notes":["Visual — the structure of Triśaṅku's \"I am\"","<table><tr><td>Image</td><td>Meaning of the \"I\"</td></tr><tr><td>vṛkṣasya rerivā — mover of the (world-)tree</td><td>the impelling root-power of manifestation</td></tr><tr><td>kīrtiḥ … girer iva — glory like a peak</td><td>unshakable, exalted being</td></tr><tr><td>ūrdhva-pavitraḥ — pure on high</td><td>source untainted, transcendent</td></tr><tr><td>vājinī iva svamṛtam — like the sun's essence, the pure immortal</td><td>self-luminous deathlessness</td></tr><tr><td>draviṇaṃ savarcasam · sumedhāḥ · amṛtokṣitaḥ</td><td>wealth · radiance · intelligence · immortality</td></tr></table>","Practical steps / observations","•  This is an aham-graha upāsanā (meditation in the first person): the seeker takes up the realised \"I am\" stance rather than worshipping Brahman as other.","•  Use it as an affirmation after preparatory purification (the prior anuvākas), not before — Triśaṅku speaks from attainment.","•  The \"world-tree\" image (later central to Kaṭha and Gītā 15) frames the Self as the root-impeller of manifestation — contemplate being the mover, not the moved."]},{"ref":"Anuvāka 11","title":"The convocation: the teacher's parting charge","sa":["वेदमनूच्याचार्योऽन्तेवासिनमनुशास्ति ।","सत्यं वद । धर्मं चर । स्वाध्यायान्मा प्रमदः ।","आचार्याय प्रियं धनमाहृत्य प्रजातन्तुं मा व्यवच्छेत्सीः ।","सत्यान्न प्रमदितव्यम् । धर्मान्न प्रमदितव्यम् ।","कुशलान्न प्रमदितव्यम् । भूत्यै न प्रमदितव्यम् ।","स्वाध्यायप्रवचनाभ्यां न प्रमदितव्यम् ॥","देवपितृकार्याभ्यां न प्रमदितव्यम् । मातृदेवो भव ।","पितृदेवो भव । आचार्यदेवो भव । अतिथिदेवो भव ।","यान्यनवद्यानि कर्माणि । तानि सेवितव्यानि । नो इतराणि ।","यान्यस्माकं सुचरितानि । तानि त्वयोपास्यानि । नो इतराणि ॥","ये के चास्मच्छ्रेयांसो ब्राह्मणाः । तेषां त्वयासनेन प्रश्वसितव्यम् ।","श्रद्धया देयम् । अश्रद्धयादेयम् । श्रिया देयम् । ह्रिया देयम् ।","भिया देयम् । संविदा देयम् ।","अथ यदि ते कर्मविचिकित्सा वा वृत्तविचिकित्सा वा स्यात् ॥","ये तत्र ब्राह्मणाः संमर्शिनः । युक्ता आयुक्ताः ।","अलूक्षा धर्मकामाः स्युः । यथा ते तत्र वर्तेरन् । तथा तत्र वर्तेथाः ।","अथाभ्याख्यातेषु । ... एष आदेशः । एष उपदेशः ।","एषा वेदोपनिषत् । एतदनुशासनम् । एवमुपासितव्यम् । एवमु चैतदुपास्यम् ॥"],"iast":["vedam anūcyācāryo 'ntevāsinam anuśāsti |","satyaṃ vada | dharmaṃ cara | svādhyāyān mā pramadaḥ |","ācāryāya priyaṃ dhanam āhṛtya prajātantuṃ mā vyavacchetsīḥ |","satyān na pramaditavyam | dharmān na pramaditavyam | ... bhūtyai na pramaditavyam |","mātṛdevo bhava | pitṛdevo bhava | ācāryadevo bhava | atithidevo bhava |","yāny anavadyāni karmāṇi | tāni sevitavyāni | no itarāṇi |","śraddhayā deyam | aśraddhayā adeyam | śriyā deyam | hriyā deyam | bhiyā deyam | saṃvidā deyam |","eṣa ādeśaḥ | eṣa upadeśaḥ | eṣā vedopaniṣat | etad anuśāsanam | evam upāsitavyam ||"],"words":[["vedam anūcya","m. acc. + ger.","having taught the Veda"],["antevāsinam anuśāsti","m. acc. + 3 sg.","instructs the resident pupil"],["satyaṃ vada · dharmaṃ cara","impv.","speak truth · practise dharma"],["svādhyāyāt mā pramadaḥ","abl. + prohib. aor.","do not neglect study"],["prajā-tantum mā vyavacchetsīḥ","m. acc. + prohib.","do not cut off the thread of progeny"],["na pramaditavyam","gerundive (impers.)","one must not be heedless (of…)"],["bhūtyai","f. dat. sg.","(of) prosperity/welfare"],["mātṛ-/pitṛ-/ācārya-/atithi-devaḥ bhava","impv.","be one to whom mother/father/teacher/guest are gods"],["anavadyāni karmāṇi","n. nom. pl.","blameless actions"],["sevitavyāni · no itarāṇi","gerundive","to be practised · not the others"],["śraddhayā deyam","f. instr. + gerundive","give with faith"],["hriyā · bhiyā · saṃvidā deyam","f. instr.","give with modesty · with (sympathetic) fear · with understanding"],["karma-/vṛtta-vicikitsā","f. nom. sg.","doubt about an act / about conduct"],["saṃmarśinaḥ · dharma-kāmāḥ","m. nom. pl.","(those) reflective · desirous of dharma"],["ādeśaḥ · upadeśaḥ · veda-upaniṣat","m./f. nom.","the command · the instruction · the secret of the Veda"]],"lit":"\"Having taught the Veda, the teacher instructs the pupil: Speak the truth. Practise dharma. Do not neglect study. Having brought the teacher his cherished fee, do not cut the thread of progeny. Do not swerve from truth; from dharma; from welfare; from prosperity; from study and teaching; from duties to gods and ancestors. Be one to whom your mother is a god, your father is a god, your teacher is a god, your guest is a god. Those acts that are blameless are to be practised — not the others. Whatever good conduct there is in us, that you should adopt — not the rest. … Give with faith; do not give without faith; give with plenty, give with modesty, give with (sympathetic) awe, give with fellow-feeling. And if you have doubt about an act or about conduct, behave as the thoughtful, dharma-loving brahmins there would behave. This is the command; this is the instruction; this is the secret of the Veda; this is the teaching. Thus should one observe it.\"","para":"The Upaniṣad's celebrated convocation address — the teacher's charge as the pupil leaves. It commands truth, dharma, continued study, marriage and progeny; reverence for mother, father, teacher, guest as gods; discriminating imitation (follow only our blameless acts); the manner of giving (with faith, modesty, awe, fellow-feeling); and a method for moral doubt — emulate the reflective, dharma-loving wise. It seals itself as vedopaniṣat — the very heart of the Veda's teaching.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","this is gṛhastha-dharma enjoined for the pupil re-entering worldly life; it is not the final teaching (mokṣa) but the conduct that purifies one toward it. The four devo bhava establish gratitude as the root of dharma."],["Sureśvara","the passage proves the Upaniṣad does not despise action — karma rightly done is the ground for jñāna; the doubt-resolution clause shows dharma is finally settled by the conduct of the śiṣṭa (the cultivated wise)."],["Rangarāmānuja","the whole charge is bhagavad-ājñā — duties performed as service to the Lord, with mother/father/teacher/guest honoured as His manifestations; giving \"with faith\" is devotion expressed in act."],["Gambhīrānanda-tradition","clarifies prajā-tantu mā vyavacchetsīḥ (continue the family line), the impersonal gerundives (\"one must not be heedless\"), and the nuances of śriyā/hriyā/bhiyā/saṃvidā deyam (give with prosperity/modesty/fear/sympathy)."],["Sri Aurobindo","reads the charge as the ethical base that supports the spiritual ascent; truth, reverence and discerning conduct are the disciplines that make the seeker a fit vessel for the brahma-vidyā that follows."]],"notes":["Visual — the charge in five clusters","<table><tr><td>Cluster</td><td>Core injunctions</td></tr><tr><td>1. Foundations</td><td>satyaṃ vada · dharmaṃ cara · svādhyāyān mā pramadaḥ</td></tr><tr><td>2. Continuity</td><td>bring the teacher his fee; do not cut the thread of progeny</td></tr><tr><td>3. \"Be not heedless of…\"</td><td>truth · dharma · welfare · prosperity · study/teaching · duties to gods &amp; ancestors</td></tr><tr><td>4. The four \"devo bhava\"</td><td>mother · father · teacher · guest = your gods</td></tr><tr><td>5. Discrimination &amp; giving</td><td>imitate only our blameless acts; give with śraddhā · śrī · hrī · bhī · saṃvid; in doubt, follow the wise</td></tr></table>","Practical steps / observations","•  This is the most directly livable passage in the Upaniṣad — usable verbatim as a personal code: truth, dharma, study, family, reverence, discerning conduct, gracious giving.","•  Note the conditional imitation: \"follow our good acts, not the others\" — even teachers are to be followed selectively; conscience is not outsourced.","•  The doubt-resolution method is a portable ethic: when unsure, model yourself on people who are reflective, unattached, and genuinely dharma-loving — character over rule-lookup.","•  \"Give with faith… with modesty… with awe…\": the manner of giving matters as much as the gift."]},{"ref":"Anuvāka 12","title":"Closing Śānti (the vow fulfilled)","sa":["शं नो मित्रः शं वरुणः । शं नो भवत्वर्यमा ।","शं न इन्द्रो बृहस्पतिः । शं नो विष्णुरुरुक्रमः ।","नमो ब्रह्मणे । नमस्ते वायो । त्वमेव प्रत्यक्षं ब्रह्मासि ।","त्वामेव प्रत्यक्षं ब्रह्मावादिषम् । ऋतमवादिषम् । सत्यमवादिषम् ।","तन्मामावीत् । तद्वक्तारमावीत् । आवीन्माम् । आवीद्वक्तारम् ।","ॐ शान्तिः शान्तिः शान्तिः ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["śaṃ no mitraḥ śaṃ varuṇaḥ | ... namas te vāyo | tvam eva pratyakṣaṃ brahmāsi |","tvām eva pratyakṣaṃ brahmāvādiṣam | ṛtam avādiṣam | satyam avādiṣam |","tan mām āvīt | tad vaktāram āvīt | āvīn mām | āvīd vaktāram |","oṃ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ || 1 ||"],"words":[["brahma avādiṣam","n. + 1 sg. aor. of vad","I did declare (you) Brahman"],["ṛtam avādiṣam","n. + 1 sg. aor.","I did speak the right"],["satyam avādiṣam","n. + 1 sg. aor.","I did speak the true"],["tat mām āvīt","pron. + 3 sg. aor. of av","that did protect me"],["tat vaktāram āvīt","pron. + m. acc. + 3 sg. aor.","that did protect the teacher"],["āvīt mām · āvīt vaktāram","3 sg. aor.","it protected me · it protected the teacher"]],"lit":"\"May Mitra be well-being to us… Salutation to you, O Vāyu. You indeed are the manifest Brahman. You indeed I have declared the manifest Brahman; I have spoken the right; I have spoken the true. That has protected me; that has protected the teacher. It protected me; it protected the teacher. Oṃ, peace, peace, peace.\"","para":"The vallī closes by echoing its opening invocation in the past tense: what anuvāka 1 vowed in the future (\"I shall declare… shall speak… may it protect\"), anuvāka 12 now reports as accomplished (\"I have declared… have spoken… it did protect\"). The frame brackets the whole vallī as a completed act of truthful study, divinely guarded.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the repetition with tense-change marks the completion (samāpti) of the Śīkṣā-vallī's study; the protection sought at the outset is declared secured, expressing gratitude and kṛta-kṛtyatā (the sense of duty fulfilled)."],["Sureśvara","the closing śānti removes any obstacles that might have crept into the study just done, sealing its fruit before the seeker proceeds to the brahma-vidyā of Vallī II."],["Rangarāmānuja","the accomplished protection is the Lord's grace acknowledged; the devotee, having truthfully studied, reports the antaryāmin's safeguarding of both pupil and teacher."],["Gambhīrānanda-tradition","notes precisely the aorist forms (avādiṣam, āvīt) against the future of anuvāka 1, and that only the relevant clauses (not the whole prayer) shift tense."],["Sri Aurobindo","the fulfilled vow signals that the alignment of mind (ṛta) and speech (satya) prayed for at the start has been achieved — the instruments are now truthful and protected, ready for higher knowledge."]],"notes":["Visual — the future vow and its past fulfilment","<table><tr><td>Clause</td><td>Anuvāka 1 (opening, future)</td><td>Anuvāka 12 (closing, aorist)</td></tr><tr><td>Declaring Brahman</td><td>brahma vadiṣyāmi (I shall declare)</td><td>brahma avādiṣam (I have declared)</td></tr><tr><td>Speaking ṛta</td><td>ṛtaṃ vadiṣyāmi</td><td>ṛtam avādiṣam</td></tr><tr><td>Speaking satya</td><td>satyaṃ vadiṣyāmi</td><td>satyam avādiṣam</td></tr><tr><td>Protection of pupil</td><td>tan mām avatu (may it protect)</td><td>tan mām āvīt (it protected)</td></tr><tr><td>Protection of teacher</td><td>tad vaktāram avatu</td><td>tad vaktāram āvīt</td></tr></table>","Practical steps / observations","•  Use the paired invocations to bracket any study cycle: open with the resolve (future), close with grateful acknowledgement that it was carried through (past).","•  The grammatical shift future→aorist is the teaching: a vow truthfully kept becomes an accomplished, protected fact — recitation as a complete arc, not an open loop.","•  Closing with the triple śānti again returns the mind to stillness before leaving the text."]},{"ref":"Śīkṣā-vallī","title":"thematic outline","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Anuvāka</td><td>Title</td><td>Type</td><td>Key idea</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Opening Śānti</td><td>invocation</td><td>Vāyu as manifest Brahman; vow of truth; threefold peace</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Śīkṣā</td><td>phonetics</td><td>the six limbs of correct recitation</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Saṃhitā-upāsana</td><td>meditation</td><td>the five Great Junctions (pūrva–sandhi–uttara–sandhāna)</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Āvahantī-homa</td><td>prayer</td><td>for intelligence (medhā) and worthy students</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Vyāhṛti-upāsana</td><td>meditation</td><td>Bhūr-Bhuvaḥ-Suvaḥ + Mahaḥ across four registers</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Antar-hṛdaya</td><td>meditation</td><td>the heart-Person and the upward path to svārājya</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Pāṅkta</td><td>meditation</td><td>all reality is fivefold; cosmos mirrors body</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Oṃ</td><td>meditation</td><td>the Praṇava as Brahman and ritual assent</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Svādhyāya</td><td>injunction</td><td>study-and-teaching as the master-discipline; 3 sages</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Triśaṅku</td><td>realisation</td><td>the realiser's first-person \"I am\" hymn</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Convocation</td><td>ethics</td><td>the teacher's charge: truth, dharma, the four devo bhava</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Closing Śānti</td><td>invocation</td><td>the opening vow reported as fulfilled</td></tr></table>","Arc of the vallī: discipline of sound (1–2) → meditations on junction, plurality, and the syllable (3–8) → the supremacy of study and the realiser's voice (9–10) → the ethical charge and sealing (11–12). Śaṅkara's frame: this entire vallī is sādhana — it purifies and steadies the seeker so that the brahma-vidyā of the Brahmānanda-vallī can be received."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Notes on this instalment","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["- Sanskrit follows the Taittirīya (Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda) recension as in sanskritdocuments.org tait.itx, cross-checked against the accented taitaccent.itx.","- IAST is given for every anuvāka; the gloss tables give full pada analysis for the verse/short anuvākas and key-term analysis for the long prose anuvākas (3, 4, 11), where exhaustive particle-by-particle glossing would obscure more than it reveals.","- The five-commentary digests are this compiler's summaries of each tradition's characteristic reading — Śaṅkara, Sureśvara, Rangarāmānuja from their Sanskrit works, and the Gambhīrānanda reading-tradition and Sri Aurobindo in paraphrase. They are not verbatim translations of any modern copyrighted edition.","- Next instalment: Vallī II — Brahmānanda-vallī (the five sheaths and the calculus of bliss), appended below this section."],"kind":"note"}]}]};
